<script>
	import Admonition from '#site/components/Admonition.svelte';
	import Tabs from '#site/components/Tabs.svelte';
	import TabItem from '#site/components/TabItem.svelte';
</script>

# Coming from React

`svelte-advanced-cropper` is a port of [react-advanced-cropper](https://github.com/advanced-cropper/react-advanced-cropper), and its public API is meant to match the React one: the same exported components, props, settings, callbacks and instance methods, under the same names. Both libraries sit on the same [`advanced-cropper`](https://github.com/advanced-cropper/advanced-cropper) core, so the state, the algorithms and the CSS are identical too.

What changes is the glue between your code and the cropper, which follows Svelte's conventions instead of React's. This page lists every such difference, with the React code on one tab and the Svelte code on the other.

## At a glance

| React | Svelte |
| --- | --- |
| `import 'react-advanced-cropper/dist/style.css'` | `import 'svelte-advanced-cropper/style.css'` |
| `const ref = useRef<CropperRef>(null)` + `ref={ref}` | `let cropper = $state<CropperRef>()` + `bind:this={cropper}` |
| `className="x"` | `class="x"` (other `*ClassName` props keep their names) |
| `style={{ height: 400 }}` | `style="height: 400px"` |
| `stencilComponent={CircleStencil}` | unchanged |
| `children` in custom components | a `children` snippet, rendered with `@render` |
| custom background with `forwardRef` | a bindable `ref` prop: `ref = $bindable(null)` |
| stencil `useImperativeHandle` | component exports (`export const aspectRatio = 1`) |
| `<CropperPreview cropper={ref} />` | `<CropperPreview cropper={cropper} />` (the instance, not `{ current }`) |
| `useCropperImage({ src })` | `useCropperImage(() => ({ src }))` |
| `useUpdateEffect(fn, [a, b])` | `useUpdateEffect(fn, () => [a, b])` |
| `mergeRefs` | not needed, not exported |
| `<Cropper stencilSize autoZoom>` (deprecated) | removed, use `FixedCropper` |

## Installation and styles

Install the Svelte package and import its stylesheet once, for example in your root `+layout.svelte`. The stylesheet includes the default theme, just like React's `dist/style.css`.

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="React">

```tsx
import { Cropper } from 'react-advanced-cropper';
import 'react-advanced-cropper/dist/style.css';
```

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="Svelte">

```ts
import { Cropper } from 'svelte-advanced-cropper';
import 'svelte-advanced-cropper/style.css';
```

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

Other themes live under `svelte-advanced-cropper/themes/<name>.css`, with the Sass sources next to them as `<name>.scss`. See [Cropper Themes](/docs/guides/themes).

Everything the React package re-exports from the core (`advanced-cropper`, `advanced-cropper/defaults`, `/state`, `/algorithms`, …) is available from the package root, so imports such as `import { getTransformedImageSize } from 'svelte-advanced-cropper'` keep working.

## Getting the cropper instance

React hands you the cropper through a ref object. In Svelte, bind the component to a variable with `bind:this`. The bound value has every method of `CropperRef` (`getCoordinates`, `getCanvas`, `rotateImage`, `setState`, …), so you call them without `.current`.

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="React">

```tsx
import { useRef } from 'react';
import { Cropper, CropperRef } from 'react-advanced-cropper';

export const Example = ({ src }: { src: string }) => {
	const cropperRef = useRef<CropperRef>(null);

	const onCrop = () => {
		console.log(cropperRef.current?.getCanvas()?.toDataURL());
	};

	return (
		<>
			<Cropper ref={cropperRef} src={src} />
			<button onClick={onCrop}>Crop</button>
		</>
	);
};
```

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="Svelte">

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
	import { Cropper, type CropperRef } from 'svelte-advanced-cropper';

	let { src }: { src: string } = $props();
	let cropper: CropperRef | undefined = $state();

	function onCrop() {
		console.log(cropper?.getCanvas()?.toDataURL());
	}
</script>

<Cropper bind:this={cropper} {src} />
<button onclick={onCrop}>Crop</button>
```

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

Callbacks are unchanged: `onChange`, `onReady`, `onUpdate`, `onError`, `onTransitionsStart` and the rest are ordinary props, and they receive the same cropper object. As in React, they only fire while the cropper is mounted.

## Class names and styles

`className` exists in React only because `class` is a reserved word in JSX. In Svelte the cropper takes `class`, and it accepts anything Svelte's `class` attribute accepts, including arrays and objects, so you no longer need `classnames` or `clsx`. Every other class prop keeps its React name: `backgroundClassName`, `boundaryClassName`, and inside `stencilProps` `previewClassName`, `handlerClassNames` and so on.

`style` is a string, as on any Svelte element.

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="React">

```tsx
<Cropper
	src={src}
	className={cn('cropper', disabled && 'cropper--disabled')}
	style={{ height: 400 }}
	backgroundClassName="cropper__background"
/>
```

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="Svelte">

```svelte
<Cropper
	{src}
	class={['cropper', disabled && 'cropper--disabled']}
	style="height: 400px"
	backgroundClassName="cropper__background"
/>
```

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

<Admonition type="warning" title="Scoped styles">

Svelte scopes the CSS in a component's `<style>` block to the elements that component renders. The classes you hand to the cropper land on elements inside the library's components, so select them with `:global(.cropper)` or define them in a global stylesheet.

</Admonition>

Core helpers such as `getBackgroundStyle` and `getPreviewStyle` still return React-style objects with camelCase keys. If you use them in your own components, convert them with the exported `styleToString` helper (see the custom background example below).

## Component props

Props that take a component (`stencilComponent`, `wrapperComponent`, `backgroundComponent`, `backgroundWrapperComponent`, `boundaryComponent`, and `handlerComponent` / `lineComponent` inside `stencilProps`) work exactly as in React. Pass a Svelte component and its extra props through the matching `*Props` object.

```svelte
<Cropper {src} stencilComponent={CircleStencil} stencilProps={{ grid: true }} />
```

They stay component props rather than becoming snippets, because the cropper needs to talk to some of them: it reads a stencil's aspect ratio and calls methods on the boundary.

### Children become snippets

Custom wrapper components receive the rest of the cropper as a `children` snippet instead of React children.

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="React">

```tsx
export const CustomWrapper = ({ cropper, children, className }: CropperWrapperProps) => (
	<div className={cn('custom-wrapper', className)}>
		{children}
		<button onClick={() => cropper.rotateImage(90)}>Rotate</button>
	</div>
);
```

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="Svelte">

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
	import type { Snippet } from 'svelte';
	import type { ClassValue } from 'svelte/elements';
	import type { CropperRef } from 'svelte-advanced-cropper';

	interface Props {
		cropper: CropperRef;
		class?: ClassValue;
		children?: Snippet;
	}

	let { cropper, class: className, children }: Props = $props();
</script>

<div class={['custom-wrapper', className]}>
	{@render children?.()}
	<button onclick={() => cropper.rotateImage(90)}>Rotate</button>
</div>
```

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

### Custom backgrounds expose a bindable `ref`

The cropper needs the background's DOM element to draw the result on a canvas. React forwards it with `forwardRef`. In Svelte, a custom `backgroundComponent` **must** declare a bindable `ref` prop and bind its element to it. Without it, `getCanvas()` returns `null`.

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="React">

```tsx
export const CustomBackground = forwardRef<HTMLImageElement, Props>(
	({ cropper, className, crossOrigin }, ref) => {
		const state = cropper.getState();
		const image = cropper.getImage();
		const transitions = cropper.getTransitions();

		return image && state ? (
			<img
				ref={ref}
				className={className}
				src={image.src}
				crossOrigin={crossOrigin === true ? 'anonymous' : crossOrigin || undefined}
				style={getBackgroundStyle(image, state, transitions)}
			/>
		) : null;
	}
);
```

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="Svelte">

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
	import type { ClassValue } from 'svelte/elements';
	import { getBackgroundStyle, styleToString, type CropperRef } from 'svelte-advanced-cropper';

	interface Props {
		cropper: CropperRef;
		class?: ClassValue;
		crossOrigin?: 'anonymous' | 'use-credentials' | boolean;
		ref?: HTMLImageElement | null;
	}

	let { cropper, class: className, crossOrigin, ref = $bindable(null) }: Props = $props();

	const state = $derived(cropper.getState());
	const image = $derived(cropper.getImage());
	const transitions = $derived(cropper.getTransitions());

	// The core returns a camelCase style object; styleToString turns it into a string.
	const style = $derived(
		image && state ? styleToString(getBackgroundStyle(image, state, transitions)) : ''
	);
</script>

{#if image && state}
	<img
		bind:this={ref}
		class={className}
		src={image.src}
		alt=""
		crossorigin={crossOrigin === true ? 'anonymous' : crossOrigin || undefined}
		{style}
	/>
{/if}
```

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

### Stencil constraints become exports

A custom stencil tells the cropper its aspect ratio (and, for round stencils, its bounding box) so the resize algorithm can respect it. React stencils publish these with `useImperativeHandle`. Svelte stencils export them, and the cropper reads the exports through `bind:this`.

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="React">

```tsx
export const SquareStencil = forwardRef(({ cropper }: Props, ref) => {
	useImperativeHandle(ref, () => ({
		aspectRatio: 1
	}));
	// ...
});
```

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="Svelte">

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
	let { cropper }: Props = $props();

	export const aspectRatio = 1;
	// ...
</script>
```

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

## CropperPreview

`CropperPreview` mirrors a cropper. React passes the ref object. In Svelte, pass the `bind:this` value itself. It starts as `undefined` and becomes the instance once the cropper mounts, and the preview follows along.

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="React">

```tsx
const cropperRef = useRef<CropperRef>(null);

<Cropper ref={cropperRef} src={src} />
<CropperPreview cropper={cropperRef} />
```

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="Svelte">

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
	let cropper: CropperRef | undefined = $state();
</script>

<Cropper bind:this={cropper} {src} />
<CropperPreview {cropper} />
```

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

## Hooks

The hooks keep their names, so you can still build a cropper of your own from the same parts. Two rules change:

- **Call them during component initialisation**, at the top level of a component's `<script>` (or in a function called from there). They set up Svelte effects, which only exist inside a component.
- **Pass getters where React passed values.** A Svelte component runs its script once, so a hook can't receive fresh values on every render. Instead it calls your function whenever it needs the current ones.

| Hook | React | Svelte |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `useAbstractCropper` | `useAbstractCropper(() => props)` | same |
| `useCropperInstance` | `useCropperInstance(() => props)` | same |
| `useCropperImage` | `useCropperImage(options)` | `useCropperImage(() => options)` |
| `useUpdateEffect` | `useUpdateEffect(effect, [a, b])` | `useUpdateEffect(effect, () => [a, b])` |
| `useWindowResize` | `useWindowResize(callback)` | same |
| `useMoveImageOptions`, `useScaleImageOptions`, `useRotateImageOptions` | called on every render | plain functions: wrap them in `$derived` |

The values these hooks return are reactive: `cropper.getState()`, `getTransitions()`, `isLoading()` and the other getters read Svelte state, so templates and `$derived` expressions that use them update automatically.

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="React">

```tsx
const { cropper, refs, image } = useAbstractCropper(() => ({ src, stencilProps }));

const moveImage = useMoveImageOptions(props.moveImage);

return (
	<CropperWrapper cropper={cropper}>
		<StretchableBoundary ref={refs.boundary}>
			{/* ... */}
		</StretchableBoundary>
		<CropperCanvas ref={refs.canvas} />
		<CropperSource ref={refs.image} src={src} />
	</CropperWrapper>
);
```

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="Svelte">

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
	const hook = useAbstractCropper(() => ({ src, stencilProps }));
	const { cropper, refs } = hook;

	const moveImage = $derived(useMoveImageOptions(moveImageProp));
</script>

<CropperWrapper {cropper}>
	<StretchableBoundary bind:this={refs.boundary}>
		<!-- use hook.image here -->
	</StretchableBoundary>
	<CropperCanvas bind:this={refs.canvas} />
	<CropperSource bind:ref={refs.image} {src} />
</CropperWrapper>
```

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

<Admonition type="warning" title="Don't destructure image">

`image` on the object `useAbstractCropper` returns is a getter. Destructuring it reads it once, at initialisation, so read `hook.image` where you need it instead. `cropper` and `refs` never change and are safe to destructure.

</Admonition>

## What's gone

- **`mergeRefs`**, React ref plumbing, has no Svelte equivalent and isn't exported. React-internal helpers that worked around render timing (`useForceRerender`, `usePersistentFunction`, `useStateWithCallback`, `useFirstMountState`) were never public and have no counterpart either. Svelte's reactivity covers what they did.
- **`stencilSize` and `autoZoom` on `Cropper`** were deprecated in React, and in the current React version they no longer do anything. They aren't accepted here. For a fixed stencil, use [`FixedCropper`](/docs/components/FixedCropper) with its `stencilSize` setting. For custom zooming, use the `postProcess` setting.

The deprecated `loading` and `loaded` props that custom wrapper components receive still work, as they do in React.

## Small fixes

A few React-version bugs are fixed without changing the API. Code that worked around them keeps working.

- `onReady` fires after the reset state has rendered, so `getCanvas()` can be called inside it.
- `isLoading()` returns `false` when `src` is cleared while an image is still loading.
- `backgroundWrapperClassName` is applied. React declares it but ignores it.

## Additions

- `AbstractCropper`, the component `Cropper` and `FixedCropper` are built on, is exported for building custom croppers.
